    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An Augustinian friar and professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, Luther protested against the sale of indulgences in 1517 and by 1521 had been excommunicated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> and declared an outlaw by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>.</li><li>He taught that justification is received only as a gift of God's grace through faith and that the Bible is the only source of divinely revealed knowledge, and he gave German speakers a Bible, a Mass, catechisms and hymns in their own language.</li><li>He called on the nobles to put down the peasant rebels of 1525 and in 1543 advocated burning synagogues and expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, writings that were quoted in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Luther is often quoted as saying &quot;Here I stand. I can do no other&quot; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a>, but recent scholars consider the evidence for these words unreliable: they appear only in later versions of the speech, not in witness accounts.</p></aside>
    </section>

