    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1483</span><span class="rf-text">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a>, in the County of Mansfeld, to Hans Luder and his wife Margarethe</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1505</span><span class="rf-text">Receives his master's degree at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, abandons law and enters St Augustine's Monastery there</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1517</span><span class="rf-text">Writes to Archbishop Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> against the sale of indulgences, enclosing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text">Excommunicated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>; refuses to recant before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> and is declared an outlaw by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1525</span><span class="rf-text">Writes against the rebels of the Peasants' War and marries Katharina von Bora, a former nun</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1534</span><span class="rf-text">The whole Bible in his German translation is published, twelve years after his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1543</span><span class="rf-text">Publishes On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Their Lies, advocating the burning of synagogues and the expulsion of the Jews</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1546</span><span class="rf-text">Dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a> after an apoplectic stroke and is buried in the Schlosskirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Martin Luther was born on 10 November 1483 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a>, in the County of Mansfeld in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, to Hans Luder (later Luther) and his wife Margarethe, née Lindemann. In 1484 the family moved to Mansfeld, where his father was a leaseholder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines and smelters and, from 1492, a town councillor. The Lutheran scholar Martin E. Marty describes his mother as a hard-working woman of "trading-class stock and middling means"; Luther's enemies labelled her a whore and bath attendant.</p><p>Hans was determined that Martin, his eldest son, should become a lawyer. He sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> schools in Mansfeld, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in 1497 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> in 1498. In 1501, at age 17, he entered the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, and he received his master's degree in 1505. He enrolled in law as his father wished but dropped out almost immediately. Drawn to theology and philosophy, he was influenced by two tutors, Bartholomaeus Arnoldi von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a> and Jodocus Trutfetter, who taught him to test everything by experience; but he came to hold that reason could not lead men to God, who could be known only through divine revelation.</p><p>Two stories are told of his sudden decision to become a monk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a>'s is that he was shocked by the sudden death of a university friend. The other is that on 2 July 1505 a lightning bolt struck near him as he rode back to university, and he cried out, "Help! Saint Anna, I will become a monk!" He entered St Augustine's Monastery in Erfurt on 17 July 1505. His father was furious at what he saw as a waste of his education.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Albert of Brandenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg">Albert of Brandenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardinal_Albrecht_of_Brandenburg_(DE_SPSG_GKI10219).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As an Augustinian friar Luther devoted himself to fasting, long hours in prayer, pilgrimage and frequent confession, and he described the period as one of deep spiritual despair. His superior, Johann von Staupitz, ordered him to pursue an academic career. He was ordained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> Cathedral on 3 April 1507 and in 1508 began teaching theology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-wittenberg">University of Wittenberg</a>. He was awarded his Doctor of Theology on 19 October 1512 and two days later succeeded Staupitz as chair of theology, the position he held for the rest of his career. In 1515 he was made provincial vicar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p><p>Lecturing on the Psalms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Galatians, he became convinced that the Church had lost sight of central truths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, above all justification by faith alone through God's grace. On 31 October 1517 he wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg">Albert of Brandenburg</a>, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, protesting against the sale of indulgences and enclosed his "Disputation on the Power and Efficacy of Indulgences", the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a>. According to one account he nailed them to the door of All Saints' Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> that day; the story rests on a statement by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a>, who was not yet in Wittenberg, and the Catholic historian Erwin Iserloh argued in the 1960s that no posting took place. Hans Hillerbrand writes that Luther intended a scholarly objection, not a confrontation with the Church.</p><p>The Theses were printed in several places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1517 and translated into German in January 1518, and his writings reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as early as 1519.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms.jpg" alt="Diet of Worms" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9B%D1%8E%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%B2_%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BC%D1%81%D0%B5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anton von Werner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> disputation of 1519 Luther asserted that neither popes nor church councils were infallible. In 1520 he published To the Christian Nobility of the German Nation, On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian Captivity</a> of the Church and On the Freedom of a Christian, and on 10 December he publicly burned the bull Exsurge Domine, which required him to recant 41 sentences. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> excommunicated him on 3 January 1521. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in April he refused to recant: "my conscience is captive to the Word of God". The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a> of 25 May 1521 declared him an outlaw.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick III, Elector of Saxony</a>, had him taken in a staged rescue to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg Castle</a>, where he translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> from Greek into German; it was published in 1522, and the whole Bible followed in 1534. Written in the German of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> chancellery, intelligible to northern and southern Germans alike, it fostered a standard version of the language. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> on 6 March 1522, he preached the eight Invocavit Sermons, urging trust in God's word rather than violence to bring about change.</p><p>From 1525 to 1529 he organised a new church in the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> with Elector John the Steadfast: a supervisory body, a German Mass published in 1526 with congregational singing and communion in both bread and wine, and in 1529 the Large and Small Catechisms. He wrote hymns such as "Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott". On 13 June 1525 he married Katharina von Bora, a former nun, which set the seal of approval on clerical marriage; she bore six children.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli.jpg" alt="Huldrych Zwingli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulrich-Zwingli-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans Asper, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> answered Luther with a series of theologians and envoys: Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case, Cardinal Cajetan had instructions to arrest him if he did not recant, and Johann Eck branded him a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>. Among reformers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> and others disputed his insistence on the real presence of Christ's body and blood in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy">Marburg Colloquy</a> of 1529, the one point of fifteen left unagreed.</p><p>When the peasants' revolts of 1524 turned into war, many peasants believed he would support them. He sympathised with some of their grievances but, in Against the Murderous, Thieving Hordes of Peasants, called on the nobles to "smite, slay, and stab" the rebels. In 1526 he wrote: "I, Martin Luther, have during the rebellion slain all the peasants". In 1539–1540 he advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-landgrave-of-hesse">Philip I, Landgrave of Hesse</a>, to marry a second wife secretly and then to "tell a good, strong lie"; his biographer Martin Brecht calls it "one of the worst mistakes Luther made".</p><p>In 1523 he had advised kindness towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, aiming at their conversion. In 1543, in On the Jews and Their Lies and a second treatise, he called them "the devil's people" and advocated setting synagogues on fire, forbidding rabbis to preach, seizing property and expelling Jews "for all time". Josel of Rosheim blamed the plight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>'s Jews on him. According to the prevailing opinion among historians, this rhetoric contributed significantly to antisemitism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>; Johannes Wallmann argues that there was no continuity between Luther's thought and Nazi ideology.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon.jpg" alt="Philip Melanchthon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhilippMelanchthon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Elder and workshop, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Outlawed, Luther could not attend the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1530 and stayed at the fortress Veste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> from 24 April to 4 October, influencing from there <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a>'s negotiations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a> presented to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> on 25 June.</p><p>He had long suffered from ill health, including Ménière's disease, vertigo, fainting, tinnitus and a cataract in one eye, and from 1531 it deteriorated further: kidney and bladder stones, arthritis and a ruptured ear drum from 1536, and the effects of angina from December 1544. Poor health made him short-tempered and harsher in his writings. His last sermon, delivered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a> on 15 February 1546, was according to Léon Poliakov "entirely devoted to the obdurate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>", whom he wanted expelled from German territory.</p><p>He had travelled to help settle a dispute with the counts of Mansfeld that threatened his siblings' families in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining trade, twice in late 1545 and again in early 1546. The negotiations were concluded on 17 February 1546. That evening he had chest pains; asked by his companions Justus Jonas and Michael Coelius whether he was ready to die trusting in Christ and confessing the doctrine he had taught, he answered a distinct "Yes". An apoplectic stroke deprived him of speech, and he died at 2:45 a.m. on 18 February 1546, aged 62, in Eisleben, the city of his birth. He was buried in front of the pulpit of the Schlosskirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a> and Melanchthon held the funeral. His excommunication was still in effect.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach.jpg" alt="Johann Sebastian Bach" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Sebastian_Bach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elias Gottlob Haussmann, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Luther's theological beliefs form the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, though he himself insisted on "Christian" or "Evangelical" as the only acceptable names. He was the most widely read author of his generation: between 1500 and 1530 his works represented one fifth of all materials printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. His Bible influenced other vernacular translations, such as the Tyndale Bible, a precursor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Bible</a>; the Small Catechism remains in use, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> based chorale cantatas on his hymns.</p><p>The partnership with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> elector was, for Martin Brecht, "the beginning of a questionable and originally unintended development towards a church government under the temporal sovereign". The Catholic Church has never lifted the excommunication of 1521, although Lutherans and Catholics have since agreed on a "common understanding of justification by God's grace through faith in Christ".</p><p>His writings against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were taken up in Nazi Germany: the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> presented a first edition of On the Jews and Their Lies to Julius Streicher in 1937, and in 1941 seven Protestant regional church confederations cited Luther in agreeing with the yellow badge. Scholars debate whether it is anachronistic to view his work as a precursor of racial antisemitism; Roland Bainton wrote that his position "was entirely religious and in no respect racial". Since the 1980s Lutheran denominations have repudiated these statements. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> Day, which commemorates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a>, is a public holiday in nine German states.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus<span>c. 850</span></a><a href="/songs/victimae-paschali-laudes">Victimae paschali laudes<span>c. 1050</span></a><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur<span>c. 1200</span></a><a href="/songs/in-dulci-jubilo">In dulci jubilo<span>c. 1328</span></a><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God<span>1528</span></a><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen<span>c. 1599</span></a><a href="/songs/joy-to-the-world">Joy to the World<span>1719</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Martin Luther (1483–1546), an Augustinian friar and professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, challenged the sale of indulgences in 1517, was excommunicated and outlawed in 1521, and gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> its German Bible, catechisms and hymns. He also wrote against the rebel peasants of 1525 and, in 1543, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</em></p>

